    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener><dateline><date when="1946-03-02">March 2, 1946</date></dateline>
                    <salute>Dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3342" type="person">Mr.
                        Morris</rs>:</salute></opener>
                <p>I have received the enclosed little article about <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__3461" type="person">Dr. Heinrich Spiro</rs>. I have
                    known him from <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__122" type="place">Germany</rs> and
                    have met him and <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3463" type="person">his wife</rs>
                    again with <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3465" type="person">Dr. Leiper</rs> and
                    the <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3464" type="person">Bishop of Chichester</rs>
                    in <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__271" type="place">London</rs> when we discussed
                    the problems of Christians of Jewish descent in Nazi <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__122" type="place">Germany</rs>. I do not know
                    whether a problem like this has much importance in American Christianity. But
                    the growing anti-semitism in this country might lead to difficult situations.
                    For this reason it might be valuable that you give some publicity to the
                    desperate struggle of <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3461" type="person">Mr.
                        Spiro</rs> under the Nazi regime.</p>
                <closer><salute>Cordially yours,</salute><signed>
                        <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928" type="person">Prof. Paul Tillich
                        </rs></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
